    description: "Per the WI-turin-pajuk audit verdict (DEPRECATE — full ADR-0023-shape migration), draft ADR-0028 instantiating ADR-0024's four-part axis-declaration template for Edge.evidence_type. The audit's cluster taxonomy (in WI-turin-pajuk's discussion thread) is the input to §1 Context and §3 Detailed analysis.\n\nADR-0028 SCOPE:\n- §1 Context: 210 distinct values across 4 axes (inference pathway, resolution status smuggled in, framework convention, apex/peer overloads).\n- §2 Decision: Edge.evidence_type names the INFERENCE PATHWAY only. Cluster A from the audit (~75 values) is the canonical seed; resolution status moves to a new sibling Edge.is_resolved boolean (or resolution_status enum); framework conventions move to meta keys; apex/peer variants collapse to a canonical 'ast_call' + meta['call_construct'].\n- §3 Detailed analysis: per-cluster fold targets (cluster B → sibling field; cluster C → meta['framework_dispatch']; cluster D → canonical + meta['call_construct']).\n- §4 Consumer pattern: consumers query (evidence_type, is_resolved, meta) per ADR-0023 §6 Phase 2 shape.\n- §5 Enforcement: axis_drift.find_drift parameterized on name_filter='EVIDENCE_TYPE' with the new registry; per-axis property test; pre-commit linter via scripts/check-evidence-type-drift.\n- §6 Migration plan: four phases mirroring ADR-0023 §6.\n\nESTIMATED EFFORT: ADR-0028 draft itself: ~1-1.5 days. Migration phases: ~7-12 days plus bakeoff validation per phase. This is the largest concept-axis migration on the roadmap (~140 production files have evidence_type literals).\n\nFOLLOW-UP ITEMS: After ADR-0028 lands, file separate items for each phase (registry, drift linter, sibling field design, consumer migration, producer migration, schema bump).\n\nDEPENDENCIES: SOFT dependency on ADR-0024 (axis-declaration template) and ADR-0023 (worked-example reference). Not blocked by ADR-0027 (Symbol.kind audit) — they're independent axes; could be drafted in parallel."  # 26e1
    fields: {}  # 26e1
- op: discuss  # b254
  at: '2026-05-02T04:55:25Z'  # b254
  by: agent  # b254
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b254
  clock: 2  # b254
  nonce: b254  # b254
  message: "**Audit-findings filing convention** (added 2026-05-02 in light of WI-rodub PR #3507).\n\nPer-cluster fold-target verdicts that emerge during ADR-0028 drafting OR during Phase 3 producer migration file as audit-findings documents at `docs/audits/<NN>-<topic>.md` (format spec: `docs/audits/README.md`; verdict methodology: ADR-0024 §\"Family-audit verdict methodology\"), **NOT** as a new ADR and **NOT** as embedded verdict tables in ADR-0028 itself.\n\nThe split:\n\n- **High-level cluster taxonomy** (cluster A canonical seed of ~75 inference-pathway values; cluster B → sibling field `Edge.is_resolved` or `resolution_status` enum; cluster C → `meta['framework_dispatch']`; cluster D → canonical `ast_call` + `meta['call_construct']`) **stays in ADR-0028 §3**. This is the equivalent of ADR-0023's §6 high-level deprecation list.\n\n- **Per-value CANONICAL / FOLD / DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD verdicts** with structured `diagnostic_test` (cmd + expect) and `rationale` per row go into the audit-findings doc(s). Given Edge.evidence_type's volume (210 distinct values per the WI-turin-pajuk audit) and ~140 production files with literals, expect multiple audit-findings docs — one per cluster makes sense. Numbering picks up wherever the audit-findings series is at the time (currently next-available is 0003 after WI-dahim's clusters, but author should grep `docs/audits/*.md` at filing time).\n\nWorked examples of the audit-findings format:\n- `docs/audits/0001-dispatch-publish-family.md` (originally ADR-0025; 13 rows across two families)\n- `docs/audits/0002-ipc-family.md` (originally ADR-0026; 7 rows)\n\nMechanical-check predicates per row's status (UNRESOLVED / PRELIM_RESOLVED / RESOLVED) are validated by `packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_audit_findings.py` and the `scripts/check-audit-findings` pre-commit gate. Both consume `hypergumbo_core.audit_findings`. When ADR-0028 lands, the registry mapping in `audit_findings._REGISTRIES` will need extending: add `\"Edge.evidence_type\": EVIDENCE_TYPES` (or whatever the registry tuple is named) so the property test can validate Edge.evidence_type-axis audit-findings docs against the live registry. The mechanical-check predicates are already defined generically in `validate_against_registry` and don't need re-implementing.\n\n**Special note on the sibling field.** ADR-0028's cluster B fold target is \"a sibling Edge.is_resolved boolean (or resolution_status enum)\" — i.e., a recurrence-promotion case per ADR-0024 §\"Fold-residue discipline\" rule 3 (N=3 distinct axis values OR N=2 producer modules). The audit-findings doc(s) for cluster B should record the per-value rationale (which evidence_type values fold to is_resolved=true vs false vs the new enum); the new field design itself (where it lives, what its enum values are) belongs in ADR-0028 §3 as a concrete instantiation of the fold-residue-discipline rule.\n\nThis convention prevents re-creating the structural confusion that produced ADR-0025/0026 (per-value verdict tables filed as ADRs in error). The bucket boundary is documented in `docs/adr/README.md`."  # b254
